The code statements that lead to the bug are:
call = rxrpc_try_get_call(chan->call, rxrpc_call_get_input);
if (sp->hdr.callNumber > chan->call_id) {
   rxrpc_put_call(call, rxrpc_call_put_input);
Reason:
When `chan->call` is NULL (because the client call has been torn down), `rxrpc_try_get_call()` returns NULL without acquiring any reference. The error path inside `if (sp->hdr.callNumber > chan->call_id)` unconditionally calls `rxrpc_put_call(call, ...)` without checking if `call` is NULL. This causes a reference count underflow on a NULL pointer, leading to a kernel crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (call)
   rxrpc_put_call(call, rxrpc_call_put_input);
Reason:
The fix adds a NULL check before calling `rxrpc_put_call()`. This ensures that the reference is only dropped if a reference was actually acquired in `rxrpc_try_get_call()`, preventing the reference underflow and the resulting crash. The protocol error handling (returning `rxrpc_protocol_error`) remains unchanged.

